---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ContactForm from "../components/ContactForm.jsx";
import siteMeta from "../data/site-meta.json";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Islands"
  description="How interactivity works in this Astro starter — when to add JavaScript, what client:* directives do, and where React islands fit."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-24 sm:py-32">
    <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">
      Concept — Interactivity model
    </p>
    <h1 class="mt-4 max-w-3xl">
      Islands are an axis, not a tier
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lg text-neutral-500">
      The three content tiers (collections, inline, site config) answer <em>where the content lives</em>. Islands answer a different question:
      <em>does this part of the page need to run JavaScript in the browser?</em> Most pages here render zero JS. When you need interactivity, you opt
      in by mounting a framework component as an island.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Default behavior -->
  <section class="border-t border-neutral-200 bg-neutral-50">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-24">
      <h2>Default: zero client-side JavaScript</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-neutral-500">
        Every <code class="rounded bg-white px-1.5 py-0.5 text-base">.astro</code> component renders to static HTML at build time. The
        <code class="rounded bg-white px-1.5 py-0.5 text-base">&lt;script&gt;</code> bundle for a typical page in this starter is 0 bytes. That's the default —
        and you should keep it that default unless interactivity demands otherwise.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-12 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-neutral-200 bg-white p-8">
          <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Use .astro for…</p>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm text-neutral-600">
            <li>— Layouts, sections, cards, prose</li>
            <li>— Anything rendered once at build time</li>
            <li>— Content that doesn't respond to user input</li>
            <li>— ~95% of the components on a typical site</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-neutral-200 bg-white p-8">
          <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Reach for an island when…</p>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm text-neutral-600">
            <li>— A form needs async validation or submission</li>
            <li>— UI state changes in response to clicks, input, or time</li>
            <li>— You want reactivity (counters, toggles, filters, modals)</li>
            <li>— You're using a third-party widget that needs JS</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- client:* directives -->
  <section class="border-t border-neutral-200">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-24">
      <h2>Hydration directives</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-neutral-500">
        When you mount a React, Vue, Svelte, or Solid component in an <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-base">.astro</code> file, you tell Astro
        <em>when</em> to send and run its JavaScript. The directive is part of the JSX-style attribute on the component.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-12 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-neutral-200">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-neutral-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-6 py-3 font-medium">Directive</th>
              <th class="px-6 py-3 font-medium">When JS loads</th>
              <th class="px-6 py-3 font-medium">Use for</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-neutral-200">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 font-mono text-xs">client:load</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-neutral-600">Immediately on page load</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-neutral-600">Above-the-fold widgets that need to be interactive instantly</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 font-mono text-xs">client:idle</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-neutral-600">After the main thread is idle</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-neutral-600">Most interactive things — non-blocking, picks up at the next quiet moment</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 font-mono text-xs">client:visible</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-neutral-600">When the component scrolls into view</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-neutral-600">Below-the-fold widgets — defers cost until needed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 font-mono text-xs">client:media</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-neutral-600">When a CSS media query matches</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-neutral-600">Mobile-only menus, desktop-only sidebars</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 font-mono text-xs">client:only</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-neutral-600">Skip server render entirely; render only on the client</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 text-neutral-600">Components that depend on browser APIs (window, document, localStorage)</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-neutral-500">
        Default to <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5">client:idle</code> unless you have a specific reason to choose otherwise. It's the cheapest
        directive that still feels instant in practice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Live demo: ContactForm -->
  <section class="border-t border-neutral-200 bg-neutral-50">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-24">
      <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Live demo — React island</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2">Form submission with async state</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-neutral-500">
        The same <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-base">ContactForm.jsx</code> used on the
        <a href="/contact" class="underline">contact page</a>. It receives the Formspree ID as a prop from
        <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-base">site-meta.json</code> and handles its own loading, success, and error states.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-2xl">
        <ContactForm client:idle formspreeId={siteMeta.formspreeId} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Vanilla JS island -->
  <section class="border-t border-neutral-200">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-24">
      <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Live demo — no framework needed</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2">Plain &lt;script&gt; islands</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-neutral-500">
        You don't need React or another framework for everything. A plain <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-base">&lt;script&gt;</code> tag in an
        <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-base">.astro</code> file ships browser JavaScript scoped to that component. Astro bundles, type-checks, and
        tree-shakes it. For small interactions, this is often the right call.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-2xl rounded-xl border border-neutral-200 bg-white p-6">
        <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Vanilla counter</p>
        <div class="mt-4 flex items-center gap-4">
          <button
            id="vanilla-counter-decrement"
            type="button"
            class="rounded-lg border border-neutral-300 px-4 py-2 text-sm hover:border-neutral-400"
          >
            −
          </button>
          <span
            id="vanilla-counter-value"
            class="min-w-12 text-center font-mono text-2xl tabular-nums"
          >0</span>
          <button
            id="vanilla-counter-increment"
            type="button"
            class="rounded-lg border border-neutral-300 px-4 py-2 text-sm hover:border-neutral-400"
          >
            +
          </button>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-xs text-neutral-500">
          The buttons are wired up by an inline <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5">&lt;script&gt;</code> at the bottom of this page section. No framework, no
          hydration directive needed — Astro just ships the script.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Decision rules -->
  <section class="border-t border-neutral-200 bg-neutral-50">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-24">
      <h2>When should I add an island?</h2>
      <div class="mt-12 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-3">
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-medium">Default to .astro</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-neutral-500">
            If the component renders the same HTML for every visitor and never reacts to user input, it should be an
            <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs">.astro</code> component. Zero JS shipped.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-medium">Plain &lt;script&gt; for small interactions</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-neutral-500">
            Hover effects, simple toggles, smooth-scroll. A few lines of vanilla JS in an <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs">.astro</code> file
            beats pulling in a framework runtime.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-medium">Framework island when state gets complex</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-neutral-500">
            Forms with validation, multi-step flows, anything with reactive derived state. React, Vue, Svelte, Solid — each
            has its own integration. This starter ships React; <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs">ContactForm.jsx</code> is the only React component.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-12 max-w-3xl text-sm text-neutral-500">
        The cost of an island is the framework runtime + the component's bundle, sent to every visitor of that page. A React island adds ~45 KB gzipped
        for the runtime. That's fine for one or two interactive sections per page; it's not fine if you reach for React for static rendering tasks
        <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs">.astro</code> already handles.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- File map -->
  <section class="border-t border-neutral-200">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-24">
      <h2>File map</h2>
      <dl class="mt-8 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-medium">React components</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-neutral-500">
            <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs">src/components/*.jsx</code> — currently just
            <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs">ContactForm.jsx</code>. Add a new <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs">.jsx</code> file and import it from any route file to mount another island.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-medium">React integration</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-neutral-500">
            <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs">astro.config.mjs</code> — registers <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs">@astrojs/react</code>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-medium">This page</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-neutral-500">
            <code class="rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs">src/pages/islands.astro</code> — pure inline route, imports a React island and a vanilla-JS counter
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-medium">Astro docs</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-sm text-neutral-500">
            <a href="https://docs.astro.build/en/concepts/islands/" class="underline">docs.astro.build/en/concepts/islands</a>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  const valueEl = document.getElementById("vanilla-counter-value");
  const incEl = document.getElementById("vanilla-counter-increment");
  const decEl = document.getElementById("vanilla-counter-decrement");

  if (valueEl && incEl && decEl) {
    let count = 0;
    const render = () => {
      valueEl.textContent = String(count);
    };
    incEl.addEventListener("click", () => {
      count += 1;
      render();
    });
    decEl.addEventListener("click", () => {
      count -= 1;
      render();
    });
  }
</script>
